# tools/cr/crushed_index_witness.rish -- a crushed index tells the truth about the room it names.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. A crushed index is the page a reader trusts INSTEAD of walking the room. That is
# what an index is for, and it is also what makes a wrong one expensive: nobody checks a page whose
# whole job is to spare them the checking. The tree already rendered one index off disk and compared
# it byte for byte (tools/g/geode_libraries_witness.rish), and that one -- 38 rooms -- reads true.
# Every other index on the shipping shelf was typed, and on `20260906` three were wrong at once:
#
#   docs-geode/README.md said "ten rooms filled" while its own table, six inches below, listed
#   TWELVE. The number was wrong the minute it was typed -- the commit that wrote it, subject
#   "fill the ten crush-empty rooms", added the twelfth room in the same commit -- and it stood for
#   sixteen days on the front door of the shelf a newcomer opens first.
#
#   docs-geode/press/README.md said "Three announcements stand", and argued the point at length in
#   a section named "The scope, stated plainly", over a room holding four.
#
#   docs-geode/wiki/README.md promised "every shipped page" and had no row for
#   docs-geode/tutorials/SHOPPING.md, which its own "what is missing" section left unnamed too.
#
# THE SAME PAGE FIRED AGAIN THE SAME DAY, one floor down, which is what turned a lantern into a
# loom. The first repair gave SHOPPING its row and left the reading one level deep, and one level
# deep is blind by construction: the wiki links `sangha/README.md`, a link entering a directory
# LISTS the directory, and the three sangha pattern pages behind that door -- 01-descriptor-exchange,
# 02-fact-fold, 03-five-primitives, each carrying its own Witness basis -- had never had a row. So a
# map promising every shipped page read green over four pages rather than one. A ROOM'S DOOR IS NOT
# THE SAME PROMISE AS THE PAGES BEHIND IT, and a guard reading one floor cannot tell the two apart.
#
# So a page may make the wider promise in its own words, one word longer:
#
#   **Kind:** crushed index of every page under [`../`](../)
#
# whose members are every tracked file at any depth under the room, minus `README.md` wherever it
# stands, since a door is a door on every floor. The walk is BOUNDED at `max_deep_members` = 256,
# the tree's own room bound, because an index is the page a reader trusts INSTEAD of walking the
# room and a listing past 256 is no longer one a reader holds. A deep room over the bound is refused
# by name rather than walked in silence.
#
# Not one was reachable by a link check, and that is the whole shape: every link on all three pages
# RESOLVES. A page can be perfectly connected and still miscount the room it stands in front of --
# presence is the cheaper question wearing the expensive one's answer (REDS %467), met here in a
# second disguise.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN, on metal.
#   Every member of a declared room has a row on that room's index. A page opts in by naming its
#   room in its own header: `**Kind:** crushed index of` and then a link to the room, reaching one
#   level; or `crushed index of every page under` and the link, reaching the whole subtree. So a
#   shelf that grows a new index arrives guarded the day it declares itself, and no list here has to
#   be remembered.
#   Every declared room is on disk, no deep room stands over `max_deep_members`, and every link on
#   a declared index opens on something.
#   A table row's Stamp cell agrees with the stamp in the file it links. Measured across all 526
#   living non-testimony Markdown pages: exactly ONE disagreed, and it was on this shelf. A molt
#   had moved press/'s Siya announcement and the repoint followed the LINK; the Stamp cell stayed
#   at the fossil, a month behind. A repoint sweep follows links, and a stamp in a table is not a
#   link -- which is why no standing meter could see it.
#   A page signing `witness:<name> GREEN` names an instrument that exists. Seven living pages sign
#   themselves; six are backed by a fixture that reads their claims and aborts on the first that
#   fails. The seventh was the shipping shelf's own front door, signing GREEN from a witness that
#   existed exactly once in this tree -- inside that sentence.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN, said plainly. Whether a row says anything TRUE about the member it names:
# this proves every member has a row and every row opens, not that the sentence beside it is
# honest. And `backed` means an instrument by that name exists and either stands on the standing
# roster or is named by a tracked file under tools/. It does not prove that instrument reads the
# claims the signature covers. The weaker claim is the one this code can carry, so it is the one
# the head makes (REDS %446).
#
# WHY THIS GUARD STAYS OFF THE SCOPE MAP. Two of its five readings are whole-tree censuses: the
# stamp reading and the signature reading walk every living non-testimony Markdown page in the
# repository, so no watch-word list could name what they cover. `standing_equipment_scope_map.sh`
# holds that absence RUNS -- an unmapped guard runs on every pass -- and reserves the map for static
# guards. A row naming less than a guard gates is the one direction that skips real work, and
# thirteen rows stood in exactly that state on 20260906 (REDS %451). So this one is unmapped on
# purpose, and pays a few seconds a lap for it.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/cr/crushed_index_witness.rish

say "crushed-index: Language EN -- an index is trusted instead of checked, which is exactly why the room behind it must be read."
say "crushed-index: Style Radiant -- four claims. Every member has a row, every row opens, every stamp agrees with the file it points at, and every GREEN signature names something that exists."
say "crushed-index: Lens TAME -- safety first: three indexes on one shelf miscounted their own rooms while every link on all three resolved, so a link check could never have found any of them."

# The counting rule is proven on real git repositories before any page is trusted to it. A rule
# proven only where it passes cannot be told from a rule that answers zero to everything, which is
# precisely the fault these readings exist to catch.
let control = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/c/crushed_index_control.sh"]
assert control.ok else "crushed-index: the control must complete"
say control.out
assert control.out contains "control_verdict=ok" else "crushed-index: the control must reach its own verdict"
assert control.out contains "a pen with nothing planted reads ok" else "crushed-index: the pen must be proven innocent, or every refusal below is scaffolding"
assert control.out contains "a member with no row is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a member with no row must be counted"
assert control.out contains "adding its row clears the count -- read 0" else "crushed-index: adding the row must clear it, or the count is not reading rows"
assert control.out contains "a declared room absent from disk is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a declaration naming a room that is not there must be counted"
assert control.out contains "creating the room clears it -- read 0" else "crushed-index: creating the room must clear it"
assert control.out contains "a row naming an absent file is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a row whose door does not open must be counted"
assert control.out contains "creating the file clears it -- read 0" else "crushed-index: creating the file must clear it"
assert control.out contains "a stamp cell disagreeing with its link is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a stamp cell disagreeing with its own link must be counted"
assert control.out contains "making them agree clears it -- read 0" else "crushed-index: making the two agree must clear it"
assert control.out contains "the same disagreement inside dated testimony counts zero" else "crushed-index: dated testimony keeps every word it wrote, and a guard may not demand otherwise"
assert control.out contains "a signature naming no instrument is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a GREEN signature naming nothing must be counted"
assert control.out contains "a standing-roster row backs it -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a roster row must back a signature -- the stronger door, since a rostered guard runs"
assert control.out contains "a tracked tools file naming it backs it too -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a tracked instrument naming it must back a signature too"
assert control.out contains "a page declaring nothing is not read as an index" else "crushed-index: the guard must reach only pages that opted in"
assert control.out contains "a bare mention in prose is not a row -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a mention in prose must not be credited as a row -- that is the fault itself, wearing a friendlier face"
assert control.out contains "a link carrying a fragment still names its file -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a fragment names a place inside a page, not a different page"
assert control.out contains "the one-level reading counts the directory as listed -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a link entering a room must list the room, which is why one level is blind to what stands behind the door"
assert control.out contains "the deep declaration is read as deep -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a page promising every page under a room must be read as promising it"
assert control.out contains "and the unlisted page one floor down is counted -- read 1" else "crushed-index: the deep reading must reach a page the one-level reading cannot see -- the two forms are two promises"
assert control.out contains "adding the deep row clears the count -- read 0" else "crushed-index: adding the deep row must clear it, or the count is reading depth rather than rows"
assert control.out contains "a nested README is a door rather than a member -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a door is a door on every floor, or every nested room becomes a false red"
assert control.out contains "a deep room at the bound walks free -- read 0" else "crushed-index: a room standing exactly at max_deep_members must pass, or the bound refuses ordinary work"
assert control.out contains "one page past the bound is refused by name -- read 1" else "crushed-index: a deep room over the bound must be refused by name rather than walked in silence"
assert control.out contains "an empty corpus refuses rather than reading clean" else "crushed-index: an empty corpus must refuse, since every gate would answer zero at once"
assert control.out contains "control_fail=0" else "crushed-index: every planted reading must hold"

# The tree itself, after the pen.
let scan = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/c/crushed_index_scan.sh"]
say scan.out
assert scan.ok else "crushed-index: a crushed index disagrees with the room it names -- read the scan's own lines"
assert scan.out contains "index_unlisted=0" else "crushed-index: every member of a declared room must have a row"
assert scan.out contains "index_rooms_missing=0" else "crushed-index: every declared room must be on disk"
assert scan.out contains "index_rooms_oversize=0" else "crushed-index: a deep declaration over a room past max_deep_members must split its index or raise the bound with a reason"
assert scan.out contains "index_doors_missing=0" else "crushed-index: every row on a declared index must open on something"
assert scan.out contains "row_stamp_disagrees=0" else "crushed-index: a row's stamp must agree with the file it links -- the link resolves either way, so nothing else reads this"
assert scan.out contains "signature_unbacked=0" else "crushed-index: a GREEN signature must name an instrument that exists"
assert scan.out contains "verdict=ok" else "crushed-index: the scan must reach its own verdict"

say "GREEN: crushed-index -- every declared index matches its room, every door opens, every stamp agrees with what it points at, and every GREEN signature names something real."
